#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ⚠️⚠️ READ THE CEILING BEFORE TRUSTING ANY VERDICT THIS HOOK EMITS.
#
# BOTH ARMS ARE NAMED PARTIALS AGAINST ONE PREDICATE: **FAILED substitution.**
# They do NOT detect substitution. Nothing can, downstream.
#
#   `date`  `pwd`  `env`  `git`  `cargo`   -- all SUCCEED. Exit 0, nothing on
#   stderr, no deleted word, no doubled space, and the spliced text is
#   well-formed prose. THE SUCCESSFUL SUBSTITUTION IS SILENT ON EVERY CHANNEL
#   AT ONCE, including this hook.
#
# Measured by Apollo, re-derived by Cally with a positive control (`db4592ea`):
# `git commit -m "..." 2>err` leaves err EMPTY -- the expansion happens in the
# PARSING shell, so a command-level redirect attaches to the wrong process, and
# a shell function cannot reach it either because the caller expands the
# backticks before the body runs. In interactive use the parsing shell is the
# operator's terminal, which is why a human noticing `command not found` was
# the only detector -- and that is a habit, not a control, and habits are not
# inheritable.
#
# ★ AND THERE IS NO SWEEP THAT FIXES THIS, because an ESCAPED backtick survives
# `-m` as a literal: a message can land SHOWING backticks and still have had
# other pairs consumed. ⇒ THE SET OF COMMIT MESSAGES PROVABLY SAFE FROM THEIR
# OWN BYTES IS EMPTY.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY COMPLETE -- ALL THREE STEPS, OR NONE:
#
#   THE TEXT MUST NOT BE PARSED BY A SHELL AT ANY POINT BETWEEN YOUR FINGERS AND
#   THE RECORD. There are THREE such points. We named two and controlled two.
#
#   1. COMPOSE     write it, do not build it out of a shell string.
#   2. RETAIN      cat > f <<'EOF'   -- the QUOTES are the whole control. An
#                  editor or any non-shell-parsing tool also qualifies.
#   3. COMMIT      git commit --cleanup=verbatim -F f
#
# ★ STEP 2 WAS THE UNNAMED NOUN, and skipping it makes the audit REPORT SUCCESS.
# `echo "a \`x\` b" > keep.txt` corrupts AT WRITE TIME; -F then lands that file
# byte-for-byte; the audit compares two copies of THE SAME CORRUPTION and agrees
# perfectly. Measured: retained=[before SPLICED after] landed=[before SPLICED
# after] AUDIT=PASS, with a positive control firing on an unrelated file.
# ⇒ THE TWO CONTROLS WERE NEVER INDEPENDENT. A CONTROL THAT AGREES EXACTLY WITH
#   ITS SUBJECT IS NOT A PASS -- IT IS A REPORT THAT THE EXPERIMENT DID NOT
#   HAPPEN. (Cally, from his own seat, correcting a rule he had sent me.)
#
# ★ AND `-F` ALONE IS NOT BYTE-FAITHFUL, which fails the OTHER way. Measured
# here: git's DEFAULT cleanup strips trailing whitespace and collapses runs of
# blank lines -- 38 bytes retained -> 32 bytes landed, different sha256. So the
# audit FALSE-REDS on any message carrying either. `--cleanup=verbatim` lands
# the file identical. The earlier IDENTICAL result at 0afe173 was luck: that
# message happened to contain neither, so default cleanup was a no-op.
#
# Only with all three steps is the hash of the retained input against the landed
# text decidable evidence -- and it is decidable only because the INPUT was kept.
# For a lossy channel the discriminator lives in the INPUT, never the output.
#
# This hook is the backstop for the day someone forgets and the substitution
# happens to FAIL. That is worth having. It is not coverage.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Refuse a commit message bearing the TRACE of shell word-deletion.
#
# WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE.
# The obvious hook -- "refuse a message containing backticks" -- CANNOT FIRE.
# `git commit -m "...\`ident\`..."` is a shell context: the substitution happens
# in the shell, BEFORE git is invoked, so by the time any hook runs the
# backticks are already gone and the message looks clean. The control has to be
# downstream of the corruption, and the only thing downstream is the SCAR.
#
# Deleting a word from `a \`x\` b` leaves `a  b` -- a doubled space mid-line.
# That is what this refuses. It detects the TRACE, not the cause, and says so.
#
# Measured on the real instance (commit d957605, which is why this exists):
#   "drops or misreads  passes both"
#   "if  always reads false the yanked arm is empty"
# Both would have been refused here.
#
# ⚠️ HONEST LIMITS, because a control whose reach is overstated is worse than none:
#   - false NEGATIVE: a deleted word at a line start or before punctuation may
#     leave no double space. This does not prove a message is intact.
#   - false POSITIVE: deliberate column alignment in a body. Use the three-step
#     form above, which is the actual fix; this hook backstops forgetting it.
#
# ENABLE (per clone, NOT automatic -- `.git/hooks` is not versioned and this
# directory is, so the hook travels but the wiring does not):
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
set -euo pipefail

msg_file="$1"

# Ignore comment lines and the diff git appends under --verbose.
fail() {
    echo "commit-msg REFUSED: $1" >&2
    echo "" >&2
    grep -nE "$2" "$msg_file" | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "  THE FIX IS THE CAPABILITY REMOVAL, NOT THIS HOOK -- ALL THREE STEPS:" >&2
    echo "    cat > msg.txt <<'MSG'        <- the QUOTES on the delimiter are the" >&2
    echo "    ...                             whole control. An UNQUOTED heredoc" >&2
    echo "    MSG                             substitutes exactly as -m does." >&2
    echo "    git commit --cleanup=verbatim -F msg.txt" >&2
    echo "    (retain msg.txt: without a file kept there is nothing to audit, and" >&2
    echo "     without --cleanup=verbatim the audit false-REDs on trailing space)" >&2
    echo "  If the text is deliberate: git commit --no-verify" >&2
    exit 1
}

# ---- ARM 1: DELETION. Leaves EXACTLY TWO spaces mid-line. --------------------
#
# EXACTLY two, not "two or more", and that distinction is what makes this arm
# usable. Deleting a token from `a \`x\` b` yields "a" + "" + "b" separated by
# the two surviving single spaces -> EXACTLY 2. Column alignment in a message
# body uses 3+. The first draft matched `  +` and refused two consecutive
# commits of my own -- both times on deliberately aligned tables.
#
# ⚠️ AND THAT MATTERED MORE THAN THE FALSE POSITIVES DID: a control whose
# escape hatch you reach for every time is worse than no control, because
# --no-verify stops being a decision and becomes a reflex. The fix is to make
# the arm discriminate, never to get better at bypassing it.
#
# Cost, stated: a deletion inside an already-aligned run of spaces is missed.
SCAR='^[^#].*[^ ] {2}[^ ]'
if grep -qE "$SCAR" "$msg_file"; then
    fail "doubled space mid-line -- the trace a shell substitution leaves when
  it DELETES a backticked word (\`yanked\`, \`status\`, \`floor\`, \`host\`,
  \`test\` and \`emitted\` all substitute to nothing)." "$SCAR"
fi

# ---- ARM 2: INSERTION. Leaves NO scar, and is the more dangerous half. -------
#
# (Cally, `93e5bcb1`, who enumerated the classes.) A backticked token does not
# only vanish. Some SPLICE THEIR STDOUT INTO THE MESSAGE, well-formed and
# plausible, with no doubled space for arm 1 to find:
#
#     `date`  -> a timestamp that reads exactly like a deliberate one
#     `pwd`   -> an absolute path
#     `git`   -> its entire usage manual
#
# A spontaneously generated timestamp inside an evidence commit is the worst
# case at this estate, where the recurring question is WHICH RUN AN ARTIFACT IS
# FROM. See BASELINE-CENSUS.md: an evidence branch already carries a report
# from a different battery, and nothing about it looked wrong.
#
# All three patterns are HEURISTIC and none is complete. This arm narrows the
# window; it does not close it. The quoted heredoc closes it.
# NOTE the timestamp branch is NOT anchored to ^. It was, on the first draft,
# and it silently passed a mid-line stamp -- a false negative on the single
# most dangerous insertion. Caught only by testing the arm rather than
# shipping it. An anchored pattern is a control scoped to where you happened
# to picture the fault appearing.
INSERT='(^|[^a-zA-Z/])/(Users|home|opt|usr|private|etc|var)/|These are common Git commands|^[Uu]sage: |[A-Z][a-z]{2} [A-Z][a-z]{2} [ 0-9][0-9] [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}'
if grep -qE "$INSERT" "$msg_file"; then
    fail "content that looks SPLICED IN by a shell substitution -- an absolute
  path, a bare timestamp, or a usage-manual header. Insertion leaves no gap,
  so arm 1 cannot see it." "$INSERT"
fi
